Derek Ray wrote:

> On 2007-11-01, Malcolm Ryan <malcolm.ryan@gmail.com> wrote:

>> I think Derek has discovered why the 'real' DevTeam is so silent and
>> mysterious.
> 
> Oh, I knew why they were already.  I deliberately decided explicitly _not_
> to be when I started this.  It will cost me in a certain amount of
> increased arguments, bickering, and what-not, but I think being accessible
> will only help things over the long run, since I can actually stay in
> touch with the people playing the game.  Even the bickering gives me
> information...  The ivory tower approach has its advantages, to be sure,
> but all those advantages lie on the side of the developers themselves; not
> of the players.

I (being a former Nethack player, now sort of a Crawl designer) seriously
wonder about this: what are the genuine benefits from shutting any
communication devteam--players? (I should note that Crawl does it in a
completey different way: we announce the changes and discuss them, and we
announce the motivation behind them. We use SF and IRC for this.)

As a developer I honestly do not see any advantage we could gain from not
talking to the players. (For example, there was a pre-release server for
the latest version. While this may spoil the excitement, it was great in
terms of bugs and balance issues.)

David

PS: the parts about the bickering etc. are completely true. We need to show
the power for implementing stuff past players' whining (and they whine a
lot :) On the other hand, quite a few of the players quickly get past the
'it sucks' mentatility and consider the gameplay interactions of whatever
they suggest.


